course: dictionary
lesson: 1355
title: A candle in the story
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A candle is a stick of wax with a string in the middle, called the wick. When you light the wick, it makes a small flame that gives light. «Long ago tunnels were dug by hand by the light of candles and lamps.» The sentence says candles gave light, so a candle is something that makes light.
explain: Read the words around candle for more clues. «Hair catches fire easily, so keep it back and blow birthday candles from a safe distance.» Hair, fire and safe distance tell you that a candle flame must be handled with care.
explain: Candle is a noun, which means a thing. You can have one candle or many candles, and you can light a candle or blow it out. To check a meaning, swap in your idea: "a stick of wax with a string" makes sense in the sentence, so your meaning fits.
fact: A candle is a stick of wax with a wick (a string) in the middle.
fact: «Candle» is a noun: one candle, many candles.
fact: Candles give light when the power is off, and they are used for birthday cakes and celebrations.
fact: Blowing out a candle pushes the flame's hot gas away from the wick.
fact: Candles are a fire risk, so never leave one burning alone.
exercise: «Long ago tunnels were dug by the light of candles and lamps.» What is a candle used for in this sentence? | *To give light | To make the wax melt faster | To keep the tunnel cool | To cook soup in a pan
exercise: «Hair catches fire easily, so keep it back and blow birthday candles from a safe distance.» Why should you keep your hair back? | Because hair makes candles burn longer | Because hair puts out the flame | *Because a candle flame can set hair on fire | Because candles like to be combed
exercise: Which of these is a candle? | *A stick of wax with a string in the middle | A cup of hot chocolate | A bright star in the night sky | A metal tin with a lid
test: What is the wick of a candle? | The metal holder at the bottom | *The string in the middle that you light | The smoke that rises up | The wax around the outside
test: Why does blowing out a candle work? | It makes the flame grow taller | It makes the wax grow bigger | It turns the candle into smoke | *It pushes the flame's hot gas away from the wick
exam: «Blowing out a candle pushes the flame's hot gas away from the wick, while very gentle blowing can bring more air and make it brighter.» What does this suggest about air and flames? | Air always puts out a flame | Flames do not need air at all | *Air can help a flame grow brighter | Air is made of wax
